<header class="page-header">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">Join us</span>
    <h1>Volunteering and internships</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
      The EISS is run entirely by volunteers. We welcome people who want to contribute to the work
      of the network remotely, at around a day a week, on the Anthology and on the life of the
      initiative. You do not need to be enrolled anywhere to take part.
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>What this looks like</h2>

    <div class="tile-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); margin-block: var(--space-6);">
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("calendar") }}</div>
        <h3>Remote, about a day a week</h3>
        <p>
          Entirely remote, at a rhythm we agree together and that you can change as your term or
          your job demands. There are no fixed hours and no set end date.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("book-open") }}</div>
        <h3>Work that carries your name</h3>
        <p>
          Contribute to the <a href="{{ '/anthology.html' | localizedHref(lang) }}">Anthology</a>,
          our record of every paper presented since 2017, and write for the EISS blog if you would
          like to. Contributions are credited by name.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <div class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon("graduation-cap") }}</div>
        <h3>Students and graduates alike</h3>
        <p>
          Volunteering asks for no paperwork and no enrolment, so it is open to you whether you are
          mid-degree, between degrees, or finished. If your programme requires a formal placement,
          see below.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2>Two ways in</h2>

    <h3>Volunteering</h3>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      This is the usual route, and the simplest. Volunteering with a French association is unpaid
      and freely given: you choose what you take on and how much time you give, and you are free to
      stop whenever you wish. You would be joining a board and a community who are all volunteers
      themselves, so this is how nearly everyone here works.
    </p>

    <h3>An internship under a <span lang="fr">convention de stage</span></h3>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      If your degree requires a formal placement, we can host one. French law only allows an
      internship that forms part of a course of study, covered by a tripartite
      <em lang="fr">convention de stage</em> signed by you, your institution and the EISS, so we
      need that agreement in place before the placement begins. Tell us early, because the paperwork
      runs through your institution rather than through us.
    </p>
    <p class="muted" style="max-width: 44rem;">
      Placements of this kind are unpaid within the limits French law sets, which in practice means
      we keep them well inside the threshold above which an internship must be paid. We will be
      explicit with you and with your institution about the hours involved before anything is
      signed.
    </p>

    <h2>What you would work on</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 44rem;">
      We will match this to what interests you rather than hand you a fixed job. Recent needs
      include recovering paper abstracts from earlier conference editions, translating parts of the
      site into French, writing a short piece for the blog drawn from the Anthology, and tidying
      the data behind the corpus. Supporting the Annual Conference is part of the year for
      everyone, ourselves included.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

